-### Octree-based Splatting
-Uses an adaptive octree structure for volume decomposition, allowing for non-uniform cell sizes. Choose when the data has irregular structures that a fixed pyramid cannot represent efficiently. [Octree-based Splatting](8067-octree-based-splatting.md)
+*   **Precision:** The error metric computation must be stable. If the volume data has high dynamic range, the error values may require normalization [G].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The pyramid construction assumes $N$ is a power of 2. If $N$ is not a power of 2, padding or truncation is required [G].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If the volume is completely homogeneous, the root node will have zero error, and only one large splat will be rendered [S3, unofficial]. If the volume is completely random, the algorithm will descend to the base level, rendering $N^3$ splats, losing the performance benefit [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The construction of the pyramid takes $O(N^3)$ time, as each level is derived from the previous one in linear time relative to the number of voxels. The traversal and rendering time depends on the error threshold $\epsilon$. In the worst case (very small $\epsilon$), it approaches $O(N^3)$ splats. In the best case (large $\epsilon$), it approaches $O(1)$ splats.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The pyramid requires $O(N^3)$ memory, as it stores $\log N$ levels, each with $1/8$ the size of the previous. The total memory is $N^3 (1 + 1/8 + 1/64 + ...) \approx \frac{8}{7} N^3$, which is a constant factor overhead over the original data [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** For binary data, using bit-packing to generate lower-resolution images can produce an image with half the linear resolution in approximately one-eighth the time [S2, unofficial]. The algorithm is designed for real-time interactive rendering on modern graphics workstations by leveraging hardware-accelerated polygon rasterization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance, adaptive resolution based on data homogeneity, efficient use of graphics hardware via polygon approximation.
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed pyramid structure may not be optimal for all data distributions compared to adaptive octrees. The error metric is a simplification and may not always correlate perfectly with visual quality.
-*   **When to use:** Interactive volume rendering applications where performance is critical and the data has regions of varying homogeneity.
-*   **When not to use:** When the data has complex, non-homogeneous structures that require fine resolution everywhere, or when the overhead of building the pyramid is prohibitive.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Pyramid construction is $O(N^3)$ [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. Traversal is proportional to the number of leaf nodes, which depends on $\epsilon$. Rendering is proportional to the number of leaf nodes.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The pyramid stores $\sum_{k=0}^{\log N} (N/2^k)^3 \approx \frac{8}{7} N^3$ samples, so memory usage is roughly $1.14 \times$ the original volume size [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** For binary data, bits can be packed into bytes to produce lower resolution images faster (1/8th time for half linear resolution) [S2, unofficial]. The algorithm enables real-time interactive rendering suitable for applications requiring progressive refinement [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance, adaptive resolution, efficient use of graphics hardware via polygon approximation [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Visual artifacts at boundaries between different pyramid levels if not blended properly (though Gouraud shading helps) [S1, unofficial]. The quality is dependent on the choice of $\epsilon$ [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Hierarchical Splatting was introduced by David Laur and Pat Hanrahan in 1991 [S1, unofficial]. It was one of the early algorithms to combine hierarchical data structures with splatting for volume rendering, aiming to achieve real-time performance on the graphics workstations of that era. The method builds on the concept of pyramidal data structures, which were previously used in image processing for multi-resolution analysis. The algorithm was notable for its use of Gouraud-shaded polygons to approximate splats, allowing it to leverage the rasterization capabilities of existing graphics hardware [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It has been cited in subsequent works on volume rendering and multi-resolution visualization [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+Hierarchical splatting was introduced by David Laur and Pat Hanrahan in 1991 [S1, unofficial]. It was designed as a progressive refinement algorithm for volume rendering, leveraging the pyramidal volume representation to achieve real-time interactive rendering [S1, unofficial]. The method approximates splats with RGBA Gouraud-shaded polygons to enable efficient rasterization on modern graphics workstations of the time [S1, unofficial]. It is a foundational technique in the family of multi-resolution volume rendering methods [S2, unofficial].
 
